---
description: Write branch-complete pytest tests and reuse fixtures when a second test needs the same setup
globs: tests/**/*.py
alwaysApply: false
---

# Tests

Cover every branch of the code under test: the success path, each error and early return, and the boundary values (empty, `None`, zero, one, many). Assert the outcome that distinguishes that branch.

- One behavior per test. Name the test after the branch it locks in.
- Reuse setup. Look for an existing fixture or helper before writing a new one. Put shared setup in a fixture in the nearest `conftest.py`, or in a shared test module such as `tests/ext/sqla/session.py`, once a second test needs it.
- Keep a fixture in one module until another module needs it, then move it up.
- Leave one-off setup inline. A fixture that only a single test uses is extra machinery.
- Mock only real boundaries (I/O, time, external services). Do not mock away the branches under test.

```python
@pytest.fixture
def user(session: AsyncSession) -> User:
    row = User(name="Ada")
    session.add(row)
    return row


def test_get_user_returns_row(session: AsyncSession, user: User) -> None:
    assert get_user(session, user.id) == user


def test_get_user_raises_when_missing(session: AsyncSession) -> None:
    with pytest.raises(NotFoundError):
        get_user(session, user_id=0)
```
